4. LAYER‑BY‑LAYER TECHNICAL ANALYSIS

4.1 Layer 9 – Human Authority (Triad AI)

Design Philosophy:
The Triad is not a command AI; it is a governor that audits, vetoes, and guides. Named after the archangels Michael (ethics), Gabriel (strategy), and Rafael (protection), it embodies the principle that autonomous systems must remain under meaningful human control even when operating without real‑time ground intervention.

Implementation Highlights:

· Ethical Reasoner: Neural‑symbolic engine combining BERT‑like transformers with differentiable logic constraints. Formally verified against 147 invariant rules (e.g., “No discrimination in life‑support allocation”).
· Strategic Digital Twin: Unity‑based 4D simulation (3D + time) updated at 1 Hz from live sensor fusion.
· Protective Reflexes: Hard‑coded finite state machines for collision, fire, and depressurisation; bypasses reasoner when latency <5 ms is required.
· Audit Trail: Every decision logged to Hyperledger Fabric blockchain, anchored to Earth.

Certification:
Designed to meet ECSS‑E‑ST‑40C (software) and emerging standards for ethical AI in safety‑critical systems.

4.2 Layer 8 – Nuclear Intelligence (QNI)

Design Philosophy:
Nuclear power in space is essential for high‑energy activities (manufacturing, propulsion) but demands absolute safety. QNI is a certified domain‑specific AI that governs fusion reactors with no single point of failure.

Implementation Highlights:

· Plasma Control: Nonlinear MPC at 10 kHz, implemented in SPARK Ada with formal proofs of stability within operating envelope.
· Triple Modular Redundancy: Three independent controller lanes per reactor (Ada, C, Rust) with FPGA voter.
· Fuel Cycle Management: Autonomous helium‑3 extraction, tritium breeding optimisation, waste handling.
· Safety Monitor: Diverse watchdog (C vs Ada) with independent sensors; reactor trip <2 ms.

Certification:
IEC 61508 SIL‑4, DO‑178C DAL A, and compliance with US NRC and IAEA space reactor guidelines (anticipated).

4.3 Layer 7 – Atomic Fusion Algorithm

Design Philosophy:
A city‑scale habitat generates heterogeneous, high‑volume data. The Atomic Fusion layer synthesises this into a probabilistic world model that serves as the single source of truth for all higher layers.

Implementation Highlights:

· Sensor Ingestion: Apache Kafka (10 GbE) with 100+ topics; schema evolution managed by Protobuf.
· Fusion Engine: Temporal Fusion Transformer (TFT) producing 10‑second‑ahead forecasts for 2,000+ state variables.
· Anomaly Detection: Variational autoencoder on FPGA, 100 Hz inference.
· World Model API: gRPC streaming; includes covariance estimates for all entities.

Performance:
End‑to‑end latency <50 ms; model update every 100 ms; 99.9% availability.

4.4 Layer 6 – Booster Fusion Algorithm

Design Philosophy:
Resources (compute, power, cooling) are finite and must be dynamically allocated. Booster Fusion is a hierarchical multi‑agent reinforcement learning system that continuously optimises allocation.

Implementation Highlights:

· Agents: One per power domain, one per compute cluster; observations include queue depths, temperature, power draw.
· Algorithm: Proximal Policy Optimisation (PPO) with centralised training, decentralised execution.
· Deployment: LibTorch C++ on real‑time ARM controllers; DDS communication with power electronics.
· Training: 10⁶ simulation years in digital twin; reward shaping for energy efficiency and thermal compliance.

Performance:
Scheduling decision <1 ms; power reallocation transient <5 ms.

4.5 Layer 5 – Hybrid Photonic‑Electronic AI

Design Philosophy:
Certain control loops require latencies unattainable with electronics alone. Photonic computing, with its sub‑nanosecond propagation, is integrated for real‑time actuation. Electronic neuromorphic processors handle slower, cognitive tasks.

Implementation Highlights:

· Photonic Reservoir: 1,024 virtual nodes on silicon photonic chip; weights programmed thermo‑optically.
· Attitude Control: Fibre‑optic gyroscope → photonic reservoir → thruster command; total latency <500 ns.
· QKD: Entangled photon source + SNSPD detectors; key distillation in FPGA; keys fed to Linux crypto API.
· Neuromorphic: 16× Loihi‑2 chips running SNNs for gesture recognition, docking pose estimation.

Advantage: Photonic compute generates negligible heat; QKD provides information‑theoretic security.

4.6 Layer 4 – Engineering Algorithm

Design Philosophy:
The physical structure of the ring is not static; it ages, sustains damage, and can be repaired or augmented. This layer continuously optimises the physical twin and coordinates autonomous maintenance.

Implementation Highlights:

· Digital Twin: Finite element model updated at 1 Hz; Deal.II solver on 32× RISC‑V nodes.
· Topology Optimisation: Quantum‑accelerated QUBO for sensor placement, truss routing.
· Docking Logistics: MILP solver (OR‑Tools) minimising centre‑of‑mass perturbation.
· Drone Swarm: 100 inspection/repair drones; PX4 autopilot; ROS 2 coordination.

Performance:
Structural model update <1 s; optimisation jobs <10 min (quantum) or <1 h (classical).

4.7 Layer 3 – Quantum Algorithm

Design Philosophy:
Classical optimisation algorithms struggle with the NP‑hard problems inherent in a self‑evolving habitat. Quantum annealing provides near‑term advantage for material discovery, logistics, and trajectory planning.

Implementation Highlights:

· Quantum Annealer: D‑Wave Advantage (space‑rated), 5,000 qubits, cryocooler; shared resource via job queue.
· Genetic Algorithm Accelerator: FPGA implementation of evolutionary search; 100× speedup over CPU.
· QKD Integration: Entangled photon sources at ring nodes; secure key exchange with Earth.
· Fallback: Simulated annealing (C++) on HPC cluster.

Performance:
Material discovery candidate in <1 week; debris avoidance trajectory <1 s.

4.8 Layer 2 – Linux Core Algorithm

Design Philosophy:
A secure, real‑time, open‑source operating system is the only foundation that can guarantee long‑term maintainability and freedom from vendor lock‑in.

Implementation Highlights:

· Kernel: Linux 6.6 LTS + PREEMPT_RT; patched for radiation‑induced soft errors (cache scrubbing, ECC).
· Distribution: Yocto Project; custom meta‑starring layer; SquashFS root with overlay.
· Container Runtime: containerd + runsc (gVisor) for workload isolation.
· OTA Updates: OSTree + Aktualizr; signed bundles, atomic rollback.

Certification:
Designed for future RTCA DO‑178C DAL B (applications) and security compliance with FIPS 140‑3.

4.9 Layer 1 – Cross‑Platform Abstraction

Design Philosophy:
Hardware becomes obsolete; software must outlive it. A thin hardware abstraction layer (HAL) enables the entire stack to be recompiled for new ISAs with minimal effort.

Implementation Highlights:

· HAL API: C11 interface for timers, interrupts, SPI, I2C, DMA.
· Board Support Packages: ARMv8‑A, RISC‑V 64‑bit, x86‑64; QEMU emulation for development.
· Device Tree: Flattened Image Tree on ARM/RISC‑V; ACPI abstraction on x86.
· Hypervisor: Xen (space‑rated) for mixed‑criticality partitioning.

Performance:
Abstraction overhead <5%; migration downtime <100 ms.

